same code to run cross-platform , on different devices. Bytecode may often be either directly executed on a virtual machine (a p-code machine , i.e., interpreter), or it may be further compiled into machine code for better performance. Since bytecode instructions are processed by software, they may be arbitrarily complex, but are nonetheless often akin to traditional hardware instructions: virtual stack machines are the most common, but virtual register machines have been built also. Different parts may often be stored in separate files, similar to object modules , but dynamically loaded during execution. ^ Cite error: The named reference Dynamic_Machine_Code was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Cite error: The named reference Jucs_Lua was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Cite error: The named reference Dalvik was invoked but never defined (see the help page ).",
